Z||000009||Gold-86SE||05-30-2000||10:35 AM||||<BR>"Were you hired striaght out of southwest?"<BR>I was not hired straight out of school, but that is because I placed myself in a different boat, yet I do know of others that were hired straight from school, because they had intership/programmer experience among other pros (like GPA).  Let me explain my situation.<P>During my time in school (sounds like I was in prison) I was a sales associate and office manager for insurance agents, and developed the desire to become an insurance agent NOT a programmer. Eventually I ended up working for three agents, the first was my father-in-law, who showed me the ropes and got me started. The second was a stepping stone to the third, where I became the office manager.<P>Although I earned a BA in CIS I did not want to work in the programming field, I wanted to be an insurance agent.  I graduated December '96, quit my job with the last insurance agent (difficulties abounded, hince my leaving). Started looking for another job with an insurance company, nothing really came forward. Either I did not qualify or I did not want to sell a particular type of insurance.<P>Anyhow, started working for the Tx Health Dept in March '97.  Programmer entry level making about 19k, not much with a wife and 2 kids.<P>As I was interviewing with the state, I also interviewed with my current employer.  My current employer did not hire me in March '97 but the state did.  While working for the state, my current employer called me in July and asked me about my interest.  Well it did not take me long to make the decision to move.<P>I was hired at my current employer for two reasons.  First and foremost the person that was "hired" prior to me, left during his first week without giving notice. Second was because of my experience with insurance, I did not have a learning curve to understand insurance terminology and technique.<P>"Was it easy for you to get on there at the insurance company?"<BR>Yes and no. Because when I was hired, I had no real programming experience and my salary reflected it.  I was shown a little mercy because of my insurance background and was hired on a long shot, from their point of view.<P><BR>"Thats something I am interested in because I graduate here in a couple years."<BR>Here is my suggestion. No matter the degree field in which you are working, you should get yourself an intership when offered, regardless of what you think you will do when you graduate.<P>Had I taken advantage of the interships that were offered while I was at SWTSU, I could have interned with Pennsoil(sp), USAA, and maybe a couple of others.  If I had that experience, I could have demanded more as a starting salary, which would have helped me and my family.<P>Although long in printed form, that is the brief history of my out-of-school-go-to-work record.<P>Good luck, with your schooling and don't let anything prevent you from finishing.  Opportunities are plentiful to those that have a sheep skin.<P>BTW, I kinda figured you were kidding around with the dodge hemi in a pontiac. Still it is an interesting thought.<P>Also, I may start planning for a weekend run with a bunch of fieros nuts, I may wind up placing a questionaire somewhere on the web asking for people's opinion, so keep in touch.<BR>||||reg||1
